course: dictionary
lesson: 5003
title: Inertia: why things keep going
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Inertia is a word for how things resist changing what they are doing. A still thing tends to stay still, and a moving thing tends to keep moving in a straight line at the same speed, until a force changes that. «Moving things keep moving the way they are going until something stops or turns them; this is inertia.» The clue is «until something stops or turns them», which tells you inertia is about movement continuing unless a force acts.
explain: «Moving things keep moving the way they are moving until something stops or turns them; this is called inertia, and it is why you lurch forward when the car brakes.» When the car stops, your body keeps going forward, which is why seatbelts matter. Check your meaning by swapping it in: "you lurch forward because your body keeps moving" makes sense, so you have understood it.
explain: Inertia also has an everyday meaning: a lack of energy or wish to move, which is a kind of laziness. If you sit still feeling too sluggish to start your homework, that feeling is inertia. Science and everyday life use the same idea: things, and people, do not start or stop changing without a push.
fact: Inertia is a noun, and it is uncountable, so you do not say "inertias".
fact: In science, inertia means an object stays still or keeps moving at the same speed in a straight line until a force changes it.
fact: Inertia is the idea behind Newton's first law of motion, also called the law of inertia.
fact: In everyday life, inertia means lack of energy or wish to move: laziness.
fact: To "overcome inertia" means to find the energy to start moving or to start a task after being still or lazy.
exercise: «Moving things keep moving the way they are going until something stops or turns them; this is inertia.» What does this sentence say inertia is? | Things always stop moving on their own | *Moving things keep going until something stops or turns them | Moving things turn into light | Only heavy things can move
exercise: Mia's car brakes suddenly and she lurches forward. Which science idea explains this? | *Inertia: her body keeps moving until a force, such as the seatbelt, stops it | Friction turning into heat | Gravity pulling things down | Magnets pulling the car
exercise: Ben has a pile of homework, sits still, and feels too sluggish to start. What everyday meaning of inertia fits? | A loud noise | *Lack of energy or wish to move | Sharp, quick thinking | Speed of a fast car
test: What does inertia mean in science? | Things made of metal stop quickly | Heavy things never move | *Things stay still or keep moving at the same speed in a straight line until a force changes them | Things always speed up on their own
test: Which word best completes this sentence? «The seatbelt stops you flying forward because of ___.» | *Inertia | Lunch | Colour | Sunshine
exam: Why do you lurch forward when a car brakes suddenly? Use the word inertia in your answer. | The brakes make you lighter | Your seat gets heavier when the brakes go on | *Because your body keeps moving forward until a force, such as the seatbelt, stops it | The car pushes you forward when it brakes
